  "markdown_report": "The best next step is **controlled residual restoration**, implemented first as a small diagnostic pilot inside each frozen BERT4Rec. At 10% research progress, proceed beyond behavioral ablations only if the pilot adds reproducible information within a declared GPU budget.\n\nThree starting insights motivate it. First, Industrial_and_Scientific and Electronics have negative-win rates of 12.25% and 11.27%. These training-position measurements indicate weaker separation, not validation performance. Second, embedding alignment near 0.51 implies mean cosine similarity near zero. Global directional collapse is therefore unsupported. Third, serving parity and position-preserving perturbations control implementation confounds. However, PAD-removal ablations block attention pathways and cannot directly support history-residual restoration.\n\nThree related-work insights refine the proposal. Zhang and Nanda [1] show that corruption, metric, and patch scope affect localization. Use plausible non-PAD replacements and individual sites. Heimersheim and Nanda [2] motivate complementary restoration and disruption. Neither establishes a unique mechanism or guarantees that hybrid activations are in distribution. Sampled-metric analysis [3] shows that sampling can change comparisons. Prefix diagnostics, pinned validation HR@10, and full-catalog recommendations therefore require separate interpretation.\n\nThese insights define three directions: measurement reliability, computational localization, and validation of diagnostic-guided repairs. The framework is:\n\n| Stage | Intervention or evidence | Permitted conclusion |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reliability | Sampler checks, serving parity, identity patches | Probe consistency |\n| Localization | Mask-preserving replacements, reachable patch sites, matched controls | Intervention-conditional computational effects |\n| Utility | Prespecified repair; unchanged host validation | Observed recommendation improvement |\n\nThree atomic ideas follow. Scores are originality/potential/difficulty, each out of ten; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n| Idea | Scores | Positive assessment | Negative assessment |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Identity-patch invariance checks [1,2] | 2/6/2 | Cheap protection against probe errors | Limited novelty; little direct accuracy benefit |\n| Controlled residual restoration [1,2,4] | 5/6/6 | Tests computation with one checkpoint | Control design is demanding; training benefit unproven |\n| Gradient-guided, matched token interventions | 4/6/6 | Could reduce exploratory site scans | Gradient rankings require intervention confirmation |\n\nSelect residual restoration conditionally. For each eligible training prefix, preserve appended MASK, absolute positions, padding mask, candidates, and eligibility. Replace selected real items with seeded, plausible non-PAD donors; retain PAD-removal probes only as separate behavioral ablations. Patch history activations only before a remaining cross-position attention operation. In a two-layer model, final-layer history outputs cannot affect MASK; MASK patches provide positive controls.\n\nFix the clean baseline's strongest negative before intervention and measure its target-score contrast. Also report changing-maximum margin separately, target rank, and hit@10 with fixed ties. Include identity patches, unrelated donors, and random patches matched for site count, layer, position, and activation-change magnitude. Separate exploratory users from confirmation users, report paired uncertainty, and correct searched comparisons [4].\n\nPreserve interfaces, hooks cleanup, model mode, RNG state, and one checkpoint per category. Check convergence first [5]. Record diagnostic/retraining costs and every attempted configuration; compare against behavioral ablations under equal budgets. No recovered intent or accuracy gain is implied. Fixed-seed validation remains vulnerable to adaptive selection [6].\n\n[1] https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16042\n[2] https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.15255\n[3] https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/\n[4] https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.13032\n[5] https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07483\n[6] https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Extend the corrected BERT4Rec diagnostic adapter with a budgeted pilot of bidirectional, mask-preserving residual patching. Use one frozen trained model per category to test which computational sites transmit controlled history-content changes, with confirmation on unused training-prefix examples.",
    "motivation": "Existing perturbations measure behavioral sensitivity but cannot locate the computations responsible. Supplied diagnostics show weaker training-position separation in Industrial_and_Scientific and Electronics, without evidence of widespread directional embedding collapse. Activation patching could refine hypotheses, but no supplied evidence establishes that it improves BERT4Rec training more effectively than inexpensive behavioral ablations. The proposal therefore prioritizes diagnostic validity, convergence checks, and measured incremental usefulness.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis), and all ModelView interfaces, including item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data, and review_data. Use view.model as the actual network. Gate patching on sampler and exported-predictor parity checks, including exact checkpoint-state identity and finite, tolerance-matched scores and rankings across short, full, truncated, and batched inputs. Preserve the existing embedding, margin, and behavioral-sensitivity probes and their definitions.\n\nBefore localization, inspect available training-loss and validation trajectories for optimization problems within the fixed 200-epoch limit; do not infer convergence from positive training margins. Predeclare the diagnostic cap, user partition, corruption rules, sites, primary contrast, controls, and comparison family. Use up to 64 seeded eligible training users per category, with one prefix per user, partitioned by a deterministic seeded permutation into exploration and confirmation halves. Report actual eligible counts; return unavailable results when required counts or controls cannot be constructed. Use public training prefixes only, with a previously unseen target and 99 unique prefix-excluded negatives. These diagnostic candidates do not replace the host evaluator.\n\nServing inputs retain the latest maxlen-1 interactions and append the reserved MASK token. Preserve absolute positions, visible-context length, the padding mask, candidate IDs, and target eligibility across each pair. For patching, replace selected visible real items with other non-PAD, non-MASK items from public training histories. Exclude the target and diagnostic candidates from donor identities. Define plausibility operationally: donors occupy the same relative recency band and item-frequency bin, computed solely from training histories; this does not establish semantic compatibility. Fix bin boundaries, donor sampling seed, and fallback rules before running. Report unavailable matched donors instead of silently changing the protocol. Repeat with a second prespecified plausible replacement rule to assess corruption dependence. Natural donor activations can still yield out-of-distribution hybrid computations. Preserve PAD-hole removal and order swaps as separate behavioral probes; do not interpret residual restoration at blocked PAD keys as content restoration.\n\nInspect the actual computational graph and enumerate exact hook locations. Patch individual history-token residual sites only when a later attention operation can transmit their content to the appended MASK. In a standard two-layer BERT4Rec, input residuals and the residual entering layer-2 attention are eligible; history-token outputs after layer-2 attention have no remaining cross-position route. Use a final history-output patch as a structural zero-effect control and a final MASK-feature patch as a restoration positive control, not explanatory localization. Never treat a count-1 position as the readout for left-padded histories.\n\nFor each clean/corrupted pair, cache reference activations without gradients. Run clean-to-corrupted restoration and corrupted-to-clean disruption. Fix the strongest clean-baseline negative identity, resolving ties by ascending item ID, before intervention. The primary continuous contrast is target score minus that fixed competitor's score. Separately report target-minus-current-maximum-negative margin, which permits competitor switching and primarily concerns top-1 ranking. Report target rank, reciprocal rank, hit@10, top-10 overlap, ranking agreement, and target-minus-tenth-negative margin to connect observations to the diagnostic HR@10 boundary. Use descending score and ascending item ID for all diagnostic ties; do not modify host tie handling. Report raw effects and zero-effect cases; avoid normalized recovery when the baseline clean-corrupt contrast is near zero.\n\nInclude identity patches, unrelated training-history donors, and random sites matched for component count, layer, position range, and activation-change magnitude. Define magnitude using the patched residual difference norm, bin it using exploration data, and hold bins fixed for confirmation; report achieved matching and unavailable controls. Identity and MASK-restoration controls must pass declared numerical tolerances. Explore candidate sites only on exploration users, then lock sites and effect directions for confirmation. Use 500 paired-user bootstrap resamples for descriptive intervals. For confirmatory tests, predeclare the paired-test procedure and apply Holm correction across selected site-by-category-by-direction primary-contrast tests; label other metrics exploratory. Report unstable effects and all attempted sites. Conditional intervals do not capture training-seed variation, adaptive model selection, or uncertainty about users' current intent.\n\nMeasure elapsed diagnostic and retraining time. With cached references, K intervention runs require approximately K+2 forwards per batch, before extra controls; this is an operation-count estimate, not measured runtime. Within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget, compare a prespecified localization-guided repair against a prespecified behavioral-ablation-guided repair with equal total diagnostic-plus-training allocations. Each candidate must independently train exactly one BERT4Rec per category; never fuse candidates, reuse other trials' checkpoints, or exceed the budget. Record every attempt and select solely through the unchanged four-category macro validation HR@10. Paired validation uncertainty is supplementary only if the host exposes per-user outcomes; never reconstruct unavailable labels or alter evaluation.\n\nCatch unsupported hooks and return null metrics with reasons. Remove hooks and restore model mode and RNG states in finally blocks. Generate interpretation prompts from measured metrics, definitions, controls, availability, and costs; distinguish observations from hypotheses. Use only histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps. Treat unavailable review fields, compatibility evidence, satisfaction, and simulator traits as unobserved. Retain full-catalog unseen-item recommendations for five real training users per category as a separate qualitative setting.\n\nMaintain preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, maximum 200 epochs, 200 batches per epoch, single-GPU execution, all four category routes, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and trial-local artifacts. This frozen-checkpoint diagnostic requires no predictor changes or extra fitted model. Any later training or architecture repair must retain bidirectional masked-target BERT4Rec without shifted-target leakage, appended-mask inference, exactly one checkpoint per category, and synchronized checkpoint loading and embedded predictor writing. Leave final held-out evaluation to the host.",
    "pseudocode": "run_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n    save model mode and RNG states\n    try:\n        model.eval()\n        run existing embedding, sampler, margin, parity, and behavioral probes\n        if sampler or parity fails:\n            return existing metrics plus patching-unavailable reason\n        load prespecified diagnostic protocol and GPU-time cap\n        select eligible public training prefixes; split users into exploration/confirmation\n        enumerate graph-reachable history patch sites and structural controls\n        with no_grad():\n            for partition in [exploration, confirmation]:\n                if confirmation: freeze sites and directions chosen on exploration\n                for eligible prefix within diagnostic cap:\n                    clean = left_padded_latest_history_plus_MASK(prefix)\n                    candidates = fixed_target_and_99_unique_eligible_negatives(prefix)\n                    competitor = strongest_clean_negative_with_fixed_tie_rule\n                    for prespecified non_PAD_replacement_rule:\n                        corrupt = replace_history_items_preserving_positions_and_padding_mask(clean)\n                        cache clean/corrupt activations and baseline scores\n                        for eligible site:\n                            restore = run_corrupt_with_clean_history_activation(site)\n                            disrupt = run_clean_with_corrupt_history_activation(site)\n                            run identity, unrelated-donor, matched-random, and structural controls\n                            record fixed-competitor contrast, switching margin, ranks,\n                                   hit@10, tenth-negative margin, overlap, and agreement\n        summarize per-category paired effects, 500-resample intervals, denominators,\n                  confirmation tests with Holm correction, control checks, and elapsed time\n        return {metrics, metric_definitions}\n    finally:\n        remove hooks\n        restore model mode and RNG states\n\ndiagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    include measured metrics, definitions, availability, controls, and costs\n    distinguish exploratory effects from confirmed intervention-conditional effects\n    prohibit claims of unique mechanisms, recovered intent, or demonstrated validation gains\n\nsubsequent_repair_comparison():\n    predeclare behavioral-guided and localization-guided repairs and equal total budgets\n    train each candidate independently under all fixed BERT4Rec constraints\n    synchronize checkpoint and predictor writer whenever trained computation changes\n    record every attempt; select using unchanged host macro validation HR@10\n    retain one selected checkpoint per category; leave test evaluation to host",
    "originality": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Combines serving-aligned sequential perturbations with bidirectional patching, graph-reachability checks, and independent confirmation under the single-model constraint.",
      "negative": "The underlying methods are established; novelty lies in the controlled BERT4Rec adaptation rather than a new learning algorithm."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "Could generate falsifiable computational hypotheses and establish whether localization adds useful information beyond behavioral ablations.",
      "negative": "No supplied direct evidence links patching-guided BERT4Rec repairs to better validation HR@10 under matched search budgets."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "Forward hooks and the existing diagnostic infrastructure permit a small pilot without retraining or changing serving.",
      "negative": "Graph-correct sites, corruption matching, confirmation partitions, multiplicity correction, runtime accounting, and exception-safe cleanup substantially increase implementation work."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "Zhang and Nanda: Towards Best Practices of Activation Patching in Language Models",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.16042",
      "contributions": [
        "Examines how corruption choices, evaluation metrics, and patch scope affect activation-patching conclusions.",
        "Motivates realistic corruptions, explicit controls, and individually evaluated sites."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Provides methodological evidence from language models rather than validation gains for this BERT4Rec benchmark.",
        "Patching results remain conditional on the intervention; natural activations do not guarantee in-distribution hybrid computations."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Heimersheim and Nanda: How to use and interpret activation patching",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.15255",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains activation-patching methodology and interpretation.",
        "Motivates complementary restoration and disruption experiments and carefully chosen behavioral contrasts."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish a unique or complete mechanism from a successful patch.",
        "Fixed-competitor and tenth-negative recommendation contrasts are proposed adaptations, not validated BERT4Rec findings."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Shows that sampled ranking metrics can produce comparisons inconsistent with full-catalog evaluation.",
        "Supports separate interpretation of training-prefix diagnostics, pinned validation, and full-catalog recommendations."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not evaluate residual patching.",
        "Does not replace or justify changing the mandated host evaluation protocol."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Circuit Hypothesis Tests",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.13032",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides a hypothesis-testing perspective for evaluating circuit claims against controls.",
        "Motivates comparison with matched random interventions rather than relying only on selected large effects."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not directly validate the proposed BERT4Rec protocol.",
        "The proposed activation-magnitude matching, user partition, and multiplicity procedure require explicit task-specific implementation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "PyTorch MultiheadAttention documentation",
      "link": "https://docs.pytorch.org/docs/stable/generated/torch.nn.modules.activation.MultiheadAttention.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Defines key-padding-mask semantics relevant to blocked attention pathways.",
        "Supports distinguishing content replacement from PAD-removal interventions."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The actual model's attention implementation and mask conventions must still be inspected.",
        "Mask semantics alone do not establish the empirical usefulness of a patching site."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "BERT4Rec: Sequential Recommendation with Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformer",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06690",
      "contributions": [
        "Introduces bidirectional masked-item modeling for sequential recommendation.",
        "Provides the architectural basis for reasoning about history-to-MASK information paths."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Reachability claims depend on the actual implementation and hook location.",
        "Does not demonstrate activation-patching-guided training improvements."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "BERT4Rec replication experiments",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.07483",
      "contributions": [
        "Reports substantial effects of training duration and implementation efficiency on BERT4Rec replication.",
        "Motivates checking optimization and convergence before allocating substantial budget to localization."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Reported runtime and convergence results do not determine costs or optimal training choices for this benchmark.",
        "Does not justify exceeding the fixed epoch or shared GPU-budget limits."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Cawley and Talbot: On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation",
      "link": "https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains how adaptive model selection can overfit a validation criterion.",
        "Supports recording all attempts and limiting prespecified comparisons."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Fixed seeds and paired intervals do not eliminate adaptive validation bias.",
        "The task's pinned splits must remain unchanged; final held-out evaluation belongs to the host."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
